[38;2;124;124;124misomorphic to ([0,1], B([0,1]), λ) where λ is Lebesgue measure.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mOn ([0,1], λ), the map S(x) = 2x mod 1 is measure-preserving and ergodic (in [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfact, it's strongly mixing).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m**Ergodicity of the doubling map:** To show S(x) = 2x mod 1 is ergodic, one can [0m[38;2;124;124;124muse Fourier analysis. If f ∈ L²([0,1]) is S-invariant (f∘S = f a.e.), write f = [0m[38;2;124;124;124mΣ c_n e^{2πinx}. Then f(2x mod 1) = Σ c_n e^{2πin·2x} = Σ c_n e^{2πi(2n)x}. The [0m[38;2;124;124;124minvariance f∘S = f gives c_n = c_{n/2} when n is even, and c_n = 0 when n is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124modd. By induction, c_n = 0 for all n ≠ 0, so f is constant a.e. This proves [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mergodicity.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mNow, let φ: (ℝ, μ) → ([0,1], λ) be a measure-preserving isomorphism. Define T: ℝ[0m[38;2;124;124;124m→ ℝ by T = φ^{-1} ∘ S ∘ φ.[0m

