[38;2;124;124;124mThe measure-preserving property: For u ∈ (0,1), if U is a uniform random [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mvariable on (0,1), then Φ^{-1}(U) has the Gaussian distribution. Equivalently, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mif X has the Gaussian distribution, then Φ(X) is uniform on (0,1).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mIn measure-theoretic terms: λ(Φ(A)) = μ(A) for Borel A ⊆ ℝ, and μ(Φ^{-1}(C)) = [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mλ(C) for Borel C ⊆ (0,1).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is because Φ is the CDF of μ, so μ((−∞, x]) = Φ(x), and by the change of [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mvariables, the pushforward of μ under Φ is λ on (0,1).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mMore precisely: Φ_*μ = λ|_{(0,1)}, meaning for Borel C ⊆ (0,1), μ(Φ^{-1}(C)) = [0m
